content.text:
Hiding the canvas gives 91 frames/1.5s (~60fps). So showing the canvas kills performance, regardless of internal size. Resizing to 960x576 gave 1 frame (weird — maybe resizing triggers full render with new layout and the test is affected). Wait, after setting display block and size 960x576, the page now has stage 960x576 but #app centers it. FPS test: 1 frame in 1.5s — worse. Something odd: maybe changing canvas CSS size causes it to re-render at CSS size... but internal canvas 960. Hmm.

Maybe the bottleneck is the drawing itself after all (not compositing). Hiding the canvas means our render function still runs and draws into a display:none canvas — browsers skip drawing? Canvas drawing commands still execute even if display none; but maybe Chrome optimizes? Actually draw calls still rasterize into the canvas backing store regardless of display. Unless the canvas is not composited, then no GPU upload.

The 1 frame at 960 CSS may be because the browser window itself is at 1440x900 but stage resized, and the canvas CSS resizing forced a huge cost per frame? Unclear.

Let's run a controlled test: keep canvas hidden but call our render? Can't call render directly (closure). We can expose render in debug. Better: in-page benchmark of our draw functions by calling them directly with a fresh offscreen canvas and measuring time per full draw. Since R functions are exposed, we can measure total time for a frame's drawing:

```js
const R=window.SAPO.render; const g=__sapo.game; const c=document.createElement('canvas'); c.width=960;c.height=576; const x=c.getContext('2d');
const t0=performance.now();
R.background(x, theme, t, cam); R.water(...); R.tiles(...); ... R.player(...);
return performance.now()-t0;
```
Draw 5 iterations and average. This isolates drawing cost. If drawing takes e.g. 30ms, then the remaining 140ms is compositing/presenting.

Let's do that with the canvas visible or hidden (offscreen canvas). Use theme from stage. Need enemies/coins etc. Write eval.

